---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import { celestial, scenes } from '../../config/images';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Elements & Modalities', href: '/explore/elements-and-modalities/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Elements and Modalities: the two grids behind the zodiac"
  description="Fire, earth, air, and water crossed with cardinal, fixed, and mutable create the twelve signs. Learn the system beneath the zodiac rather than memorizing twelve disconnected personalities."
  path="/explore/elements-and-modalities/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>Elements and modalities</h1>
        <p class="lede">The twelve signs make much more sense when you see the system underneath them. Four elements crossed with three modalities produce twelve distinct combinations, one for each sign.</p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={scenes.signs.src}
        srcset={scenes.signs.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={scenes.signs.alt}
        width={scenes.signs.width}
        height={scenes.signs.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Two coordinates, twelve combinations</h2>
      <p>
        Each sign belongs to one <em>element</em> and one <em>modality</em>. No two signs share the
        same combination. Aries is cardinal fire, Taurus fixed earth, Gemini mutable air, and so on.
        This structure is one of the reasons I prefer teaching the zodiac as a system instead of as
        twelve separate lists of personality traits.
      </p>

      <h2>The four elements</h2>
      <p>Elements describe broad modes of engagement and emphasis.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Fire</strong> (Aries, Leo, Sagittarius): animation, initiative, confidence,
          enthusiasm, and the impulse to move toward experience.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Earth</strong> (Taurus, Virgo, Capricorn): material reality, practicality,
          embodiment, maintenance, and attention to what can be made useful or durable.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Air</strong> (Gemini, Libra, Aquarius): thought, exchange, comparison, language,
          relationship among ideas, and the ability to gain perspective.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Water</strong> (Cancer, Scorpio, Pisces): feeling, receptivity, attachment,
          intuition, memory, and sensitivity to what is happening beneath the surface.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These are symbolic categories, not personality verdicts. A chart with strong fire does not
        automatically describe an impulsive person, and a chart with less earth does not prove
        someone is impractical. Planets, houses, aspects, rulerships, and lived experience all add
        context.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.seedlings} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>The three modalities</h2>
      <p>
        Modalities describe how a sign tends to move through a process. They also correspond to a
        sign's position within a season.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Cardinal</strong> (Aries, Cancer, Libra, Capricorn): initiating, directing,
          beginning, and setting something in motion.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fixed</strong> (Taurus, Leo, Scorpio, Aquarius): sustaining, stabilizing,
          concentrating, deepening, and holding a course.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Mutable</strong> (Gemini, Virgo, Sagittarius, Pisces): adapting, distributing,
          translating, changing form, and preparing for transition.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Signs sharing a modality form squares and oppositions to one another around the zodiac. That
        geometric relationship is part of why they may describe different ways of approaching the
        same kind of task: cardinal signs all initiate, for example, but they do so through four very
        different elements.
      </p>

      <h2>Putting the grids together</h2>
      <p>
        This is where the system becomes memorable. Scorpio is fixed water: water's emotional depth
        operating through fixed concentration. Gemini is mutable air: air's exchange of ideas moving
        through adaptation. Capricorn is cardinal earth: material reality approached through
        initiation and direction.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can also look at elemental and modal emphasis across a whole chart, but I would resist
        turning the count into a diagnosis. A pattern of fire, earth, air, water, cardinal, fixed,
        and mutable placements becomes meaningful when we ask which planets are carrying that
        emphasis, where they are placed, and how the person actually lives it. Structure first;
        synthesis next.
      </p>
      <p>
        Next: <a href="/explore/signs/">the twelve signs one by one</a>, or
        <a href="/explore/aspects/">how aspects work</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="See the system, then see the individual"
    body="A natal reading uses these patterns as a starting point and then asks how they combine in your particular chart and life."
    buttonLabel="See the readings"
    buttonHref="/readings/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
